   there keeps its "osm:" source reference and stays under the ODbL.

     https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/

   What the ODbL does and does not cover:
   - It covers variants_osm.json (and any copy or changed version of it). If you
     redistribute that file, or a database made from it, keep it under the ODbL,
     keep the attribution, and offer the changed database if you publish one.
   - It does not cover the other files. The boundaries, places.json,
     variants.json, the normalisation rules and the test cases are independent
     databases kept beside the OSM file, so the packages are a "Collective
     Database" (ODbL section 4.5) and those files keep their own licences
     (CC BY 3.0 IGO and CC BY 4.0).
   - Matching results (the P-codes and names returned for your data) are, at
     most, a "Produced Work" (ODbL section 4.3): they are not under the ODbL and
     need no share-alike. When a published result relies substantially on the
     OpenStreetMap spellings, add "Contains information from OpenStreetMap,
     available under the ODbL".

